<Ui xmlns="http://www.blizzard.com/wow/ui/"
	xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.blizzard.com/wow/FrameXML/UI.xsd">

	<Script file="KeyCapture.lua" />

	<!--
		Asking for one key, for one action or one key group. The reasoning is in `KeyCapture.lua`;
		what is settled here is the shape.

		**This one does not move, and `OnLoad` is where that is said** - `SetMovable(false)` before
		`InitDialog`, which reads it. Every other dialog is dragged from anywhere on itself because
		the chrome has no title bar to grab, and here that gesture and the left mouse button being a
		perfectly good binding are the same gesture: a drag ends in a release on this frame, so
		moving the dialog would bind the button that moved it. Written in Lua rather than as
		`movable="false"` here because the frame inherits `movable="true"` and what an attribute
		does against an inherited one is not something this repo has measured - and the failure is
		the quiet kind, one drag that silently becomes a binding.

		**`enableKeyboard`, and never turned off.** A hidden frame receives nothing, so the state
		this dialog has to keep is only "am I up" - which is what `IsShown` already is. The mode has
		to switch its listening on and off because its target frame stays on screen either way.

		**Only the width is written here.** The height is added up per open, because the dialog grows
		with the set it is asking about - `DebindBringFrame` is the other one that does this and
		carries the same note. 380 wide leaves 300 inside `ContentArea`, which is the 120+120 button
		pair straddling the middle plus its gap.
	-->
	<Frame name="DebindKeyCaptureFrame" inherits="DebindDialogTemplate" mixin="DebindKeyCaptureFrameMixin"
		enableKeyboard="true">
		<Size x="380" y="150"/>
		<Frames>
			<!--
				The pair the other two dialogs already use: 120x22 straddling `ContentArea`'s bottom,
				5 either side, the way out on the left and the thing that acts on the right
				(`DebindPasteFrame`, `DebindBringFrame`).

				Both take client globals, so both are already in every language and follow the game
				if it ever rewords them. `UNBIND` is the client's own "Unbind Key" - the shorter
				"Unbind" would be a string of ours to translate everywhere for nothing, and the word
				it drops is the one that says the button acts on the key already on those rows rather
				than on the one about to be pressed.

				**Cancel, not Close, with nothing to cancel.** The two are the same action here - the
				dialog commits and closes on the first key, so there is never a change waiting to be
				undone. What decides it is the button next to it: against [Unbind Key], which does
				take the key away, "Cancel" says "leave it as it is" and "Close" says nothing at all.
				A dialog that asks a question is also where the reader has already been taught that
				the second button refuses it.
			-->
			<Button parentKey="CancelButton" inherits="UIPanelButtonTemplate" text="CANCEL">
				<Size x="120" y="22"/>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT" relativeKey="$parent.ContentArea" relativePoint="BOTTOM" x="5" y="0"/>
				</Anchors>
			</Button>

			<Button parentKey="UnbindButton" inherits="UIPanelButtonTemplate" text="UNBIND">
				<Size x="120" y="22"/>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT" relativeKey="$parent.ContentArea" relativePoint="BOTTOM" x="-5" y="0"/>
				</Anchors>
			</Button>
		</Frames>

		<!--
			Top to bottom: what to press, where the mouse counts, the key these are on today, then
			the actions themselves.

			Three weights, because a dialog in one colour is a dialog nobody reads a shape out of -
			the label gold, the reading white, the aside grey. The paste dialog captions its fields
			in gold already, and this window's standing rule is that gold is a label or something
			pressable and never the value itself.

			**The key is written once, over the set.** A key group *is* the actions that share one
			key, so a key column down the rows would print the same string on every one of them.

			**Every vertical position is Lua's** (`Open`), because the stack above the rows has a
			wrapped paragraph in it and the stack below has however many rows the group has. Only the
			relationships that never move are written here: `CurrentKey` follows its label, and the
			description spans the content width so it has something to wrap inside.

			The rows are built in Lua too. They are plain regions rather than buttons on purpose - a
			frame that takes the mouse would swallow the click this dialog exists to read.
		-->
		<Layers>
			<Layer level="ARTWORK">
				<!--
					**What to press, and where the mouse counts.** The title names the act; this says
					how to perform it, including the one rule the reader cannot guess - a mouse
					button or the wheel is only read where it lands, and landing anywhere else does
					what it always does.

					Full size, because it is the body of the dialog. It was `Small` while it was a
					grey footnote under the list, and the size came along when it moved up here.
				-->
				<FontString parentKey="Description" inherits="GameFontHighlight" justifyH="LEFT" wordwrap="true"/>

				<FontString parentKey="CurrentKeyLabel" inherits="GameFontNormal" justifyH="LEFT" maxLines="1"/>
				<!--
					Declared after the label it follows: a `relativeKey` naming something not built
					yet is dropped without a word (`tools/check-xml-anchors.js`).
				-->
				<FontString parentKey="CurrentKey" inherits="GameFontHighlight" justifyH="LEFT" maxLines="1">
					<Anchors>
						<Anchor point="LEFT" relativeKey="$parent.CurrentKeyLabel" relativePoint="RIGHT" x="4" y="0"/>
					</Anchors>
				</FontString>

				<FontString parentKey="TargetsLabel" inherits="GameFontNormal" justifyH="LEFT" maxLines="1"/>
			</Layer>
		</Layers>
		<Scripts>
			<OnLoad method="OnLoad"/>
			<OnShow method="OnShow"/>
			<OnHide method="OnHide"/>
			<OnKeyDown method="OnKeyDown"/>
			<OnMouseDown method="OnMouseDown"/>
			<OnMouseUp method="OnMouseUp"/>
			<OnMouseWheel method="OnMouseWheel"/>
		</Scripts>
	</Frame>

</Ui>
